To verify the normalization procedure, the pre‐ and post‐normalized intensity distributions can be plotted as boxplots, which are expected to align around similar intensity distributions after normalization. A similar approach can be taken to visualize either decoy hits or imputed values, illustrating their distributions along the total peptide distribution using histograms and separately marking them in the visualization to see if their distribution fits with the expectation before proceeding with the analysis [
4
]. The coefficient of variance (CV) distributions between conditions and peptide fold change distributions can also contribute to the evaluation of the quality and consistency of the sample preparation and data processing (Figure
4A,B
). While a single value for the median or average CV is occasionally sufficient, it can be beneficial to verify that the distributions look as expected. Exact values will generally depend on the workflow and instrumentation used, but in general, good datasets will have normally distributed CVs with a median around 10%–15%, and fold change distributions for samples with comparatively high protease activity will visibly tail off towards one of the conditions due to the differential cleavages induced by the protease. It is also expected that higher CV values are from lower‐abundant peptides, which can be visualized with a scatterplot of peptide intensity versus CV. With a similar strategy, a plot of peptide or protein intensity versus intensity rank can illustrate the dynamic range of the experiment.
